module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.emitter_index_keyed_on_a_type_leaf_takes_the_last_declarer

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data emitter_index_keyed_on_a_type_leaf_takes_the_last_declarer: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "emitter_index_keyed_on_a_type_leaf_takes_the_last_declarer" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "an emitter index keyed on a type's leaf takes the last declarer: the Rust emitter builds its per-closure facts about type declarations into maps keyed on the declared LEAF NAME, folded over every module with map_insert, so when two modules of one closure declare the same leaf the map holds ONE row -- whichever was folded last -- and every reader of either declaration is answered with that row. INVALID STATE: a declaration emitted from another declaration's facts. Nothing refuses: the lookup is Present, the row is well-formed, and it is about a different type.",

    "HARM, IN TWO GRADES. Where the foreign fact contradicts the Rust the declaration needs, rustc refuses and the closure does not build -- loud, but located at the emitted field, not at the collision. Where the foreign fact is merely DIFFERENT -- a sharing decision, a variant-to-enum owner, a field's access style, a derive set that still compiles -- the emitted program builds and behaves as the other declaration's facts dictate, which is the silent grade and the reason this is a row and not a build fix.",

    "SPECIMEN, the native approval broker closure (entry dag/gunbc/auth/approval_broker_serve.dag, lane 2A of the native-broker program, main fd30a502): std.access declares AccessRequest<S, A, O, C, E> and gunbc.auth.access_request declares AccessRequest<Subject>. v1.compiler.infer_emit_info add_emit_item_summary inserted both under `AccessRequest`; v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit v1_clone_bounded_type_params therefore held the one-element set Subject for that key, v1_item_clone_bounded_param_names filtered std.access's own [S, A, O, C, E] against it and found none, and std_access.rs printed `pub struct AccessRequest<S, A, O, C, E>` over fields naming AccessContext<C: Clone> and AccessEvidence<E: Clone>: ten rustc errors (E0277, E0369). The paired control is the same emitter over dag/std/authorization_profile.dag, whose closure has one AccessRequest and prints all five bounds.",

    "WHAT IS CLOSED AND WHAT IS NOT. The trait-derive family is re-keyed on the declaration -- v1.compiler.infer_emit_info TypeDeclIndex (EmitGraphInfo.type_decl_items), and with it clone_bounded_type_params, clone_impl_required_type_params and map_key_required_type_names -- under the item registry's own identity (v1.std.core callable_identity over owner module and declared name), and a reference reaches a row through resolve_type_decl_routed, whose spelling arm REFUSES a leaf two modules declare. RE-KEYED IN A SECOND CUT: type_summaries is a TypeSummaryIndex keyed by the same declaration identity (variant-payload rows under `<identity>::<Variant>`), and variant_to_enum maps each variant to its owner's identity, so a second same-leaf declaration no longer replaces the first's summary for readers in other modules (the unit row same_leaf_type_elsewhere_leaves_other_modules_emission_unchanged). Readers that hold only a NAME read it through type_summary_decided, which answers no summary for a leaf two declarations own; that residue ends when TypeSummary's field_type_map and field_import_surface_names store identities. STILL LEAF-KEYED, LAST WRITE WINS, on v1.compiler.infer_emit_info EmitGraphInfo: fn_decl_items, recursive_type_set, fielded_variants, positional_payload_variants and shared_types. data_variant_wire_spellings is keyed on the coproduct's leaf too but stores DataVariantSpellingRefused when two declarers disagree, so it fails closed; ownership_index and read_only_params_index are module-qualified and are not members.",

    "A DECLARED MIDDLE STATE INSIDE THE CLOSED PART: kernel spellings. A kernel spelling (std.types is_kernel_type) is a reference resolve deliberately leaves unstamped, and the corpus also declares some of those leaves in two modules: v2.std.text and v2.std.node String, std.integer and v2.std.integer Int. Bool was a third until v2.std.logic Bool was retired; the arm is about any kernel-spelled leaf with two declarers, not about a fixed list. For such a reference the routed resolution answers TypeDeclKernelSpellingUndecided: it does NOT refuse, it derives no Clone or Ord bound from either declaration, and it carries a map-key requirement to EVERY declarer of the leaf, which is what the leaf-keyed set did. So for these leaves the emitted derives are unchanged by the re-keying and the question of which declaration a kernel-spelled position denotes is still open. Each occurrence is one row of v1.tests.claim.carrier_realization_census (emitter_decl_route). TRIGGER, at capability grain: resolve identifies a kernel-spelled reference -- as the kernel type or as the corpus declaration its scope binds -- at every type position the emitter reads; the owner is the text-boundary identity work (gunbc.rung_drop text_boundary_identity_wall), not the emitter. One stamped position does not retire the arm.",

    "RUNG: for the trait-derive family, mechanically preventable -- gunbc.instruments.native_emission_controls (two ControlSlot declarations of different arity in one closure, emitted, built and run) and the unit row same_leaf_type_declarations_emit_their_own_bounds are the discriminating red and the positive control. For the five indexes still leaf-keyed, mitigatable at best: the collision is observed only when rustc happens to refuse the foreign fact. ATTAINABLE CEILING: structurally impossible -- an index keyed on the declaration has no row two declarations can share. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, at capability grain: every EmitGraphInfo index that answers a question about a type declaration is keyed on the declaration's identity and reached from a reference through the one routed resolution, so that adding a same-leaf declaration to any module of a closure cannot change what is emitted for an existing one. Re-keying one more map is not that capability; the trigger is the set.",
    "OCCURRENCE, 2026-10-22 (snappy-pike-314, Charter 1 emitter tripwire; repaired in gunbc#14816): THE SHARED SET IS LEAF-KEYED AND BIT Word64 DERIVED Copy OVER Rc<Vec<Rc<Byte>>> (rustc E0204). v1.compiler.emit_rust build_shared_types marked a leaf shared only when every declarer would be (shared_by_every_declarer), and Copy eligibility was membership of that set, so std.machine_word Word64 (a constant record) and std.bit Word64 (a heap record) read as one unshared leaf. REPAIR, gunbc#14816: the declaration-site decisions (derive set Copy versus heap, and the fn-field serde narrowing) are keyed on type_decl_identity through type_declaration_is_shared and type_declaration_has_fn_fields, and v1_emit_struct_derives receives the declaration's own answer as a Bool instead of the leaf-keyed set. Executed pair: the fixture dag/test/fixture/derive_identity_collision (two Word64, one value, one heap) emits and builds with the change and is E0204 without it.",

    "CENSUS OF THE SPELLING-ONLY READERS, before and after gunbc#14816: set_contains(shared_types, <name>) was 26 in 05_emit_rust.dag, 3 in trait_derive_emit.dag and 1 in 05_emit.dag. The 3 in trait_derive_emit.dag are converted (0 remain). Remaining: 26 lines in 05_emit_rust.dag (2 of them comment lines; the rest use-site Rc wrap, constructor, field, scrutinee and clone readers), 1 in 05_emit.dag, plus 2 expression-site type_has_fn_fields readers and the infer-side recursive_type_set. DECLARED RESIDUE, not closed: a use site reads sharing by leaf.",

    "REAL-POPULATION CONTROL for the residue: leaf pairs admitted in the gate closures whose declarers disagree on sharing are NonNegativeInt/PositiveInt, OccurrenceBinding and SourceSpan (OccurrenceId does NOT collide). They are the population on which a use-site leaf disagreement is exercised by the real gates; rustc is the refusing consumer when a use-site wrap disagrees with a declaration.",

    "NEXT WALL, recorded not fixed: after the std.bit Word64 deletion the closure native-materialization-store-closure refuses at E0282 in std_list.rs:44:37 (type annotations needed). RUNG after gunbc#14816: declaration-site derive family mechanically preventable (executed RED and positive control on the fixture); use-site sharing readers mitigatable (loud at rustc, no silent grade shown). ATTAINABLE CEILING: structurally impossible. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, AT CAPABILITY GRAIN: every emit index and renderer that answers a sharing question is keyed on the declaration identity, SUFFICIENT FOR two same-leaf declarations that disagree on sharing to both emit and build in one closure.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_emit_info", decl_name: "TypeDeclIndex"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_emit_info", decl_name: "resolve_type_decl_routed"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_emit_info", decl_name: "EmitGraphInfo"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.native_emission_controls", decl_name: "same_leaf_declaration_cases"),
  ],
}
